00:35:44,721 --> 00:35:48,424
now is electric propulsion systems. Just like every other mode of transportation,

492
00:35:49,162 --> 00:35:52,867
aviation is embracing electric technologies. And just like every other

493
00:35:53,367 --> 00:35:56,926
mode of transportation, we're also struggling with the issues that surface modes

494
00:35:56,942 --> 00:36:00,327
struggled with— range anxieties, where's the infrastructure, how do

495
00:36:00,827 --> 00:36:04,065
we recharge, where are we going to recharge. new firefighting capabilities,

496
00:36:04,177 --> 00:36:07,562
responding to instances and things of that nature. And so we're learning from the ground

497
00:36:08,062 --> 00:36:11,734
and we're, you know, having a new— enjoying new lines of communications from

498
00:36:11,798 --> 00:36:14,974
other modes of transportation and cross-pollinating new ideas

499
00:36:15,087 --> 00:36:18,841
on how we're addressing these challenges as we're going forward. And we're

500
00:36:19,341 --> 00:36:23,477
also learning about hybrid technologies, extending ranges through those capabilities

501
00:36:23,525 --> 00:36:27,376
and whatnot. And even hydrogen is starting to break into some conversations both

502
00:36:27,876 --> 00:36:30,633
in the air as a part of the aircraft systems, but also on the ground

503
00:36:31,133 --> 00:36:34,724
as part of the infrastructure and how we manage and charge aircraft and

504
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:38,285
do other things. The second bucket that characterizes advanced

505
00:36:38,785 --> 00:36:42,296
air mobility is automation and autonomy. And this is something that is scary for

506
00:36:42,796 --> 00:36:46,179
a lot of people when you think about aircraft moving through the air, but aviation

507
00:36:46,435 --> 00:36:50,029
has embraced autonomy going back to the late '60s and into the '70s

508
00:36:50,783 --> 00:36:54,057
when we had a string of midair collisions that could have been avoided were

509
00:36:54,557 --> 00:36:57,777
caused by human error from pilots being oversaturated with tasks

510
00:36:58,277 --> 00:37:02,290
during difficult phases of flight. We introduced autonomy through autopilot,

511
00:37:04,680 --> 00:37:08,093
ground traffic avoidance systems, traffic collision avoidance

512
00:37:08,158 --> 00:37:12,376
systems, and those, you know, when we put those in the right places, it reduced

513
00:37:12,521 --> 00:37:16,206
the occurrence of those types of collisions and, you know, eventually made

514
00:37:16,238 --> 00:37:19,535
air traffic and airline travel the safest mode of transportation in

515
00:37:20,035 --> 00:37:24,523
the world within the United States. And so we've been incrementally adding autonomy to

516
00:37:25,023 --> 00:37:29,283
different parts of aviation, you know, since the '60s and '70s to today, where if

517
00:37:29,331 --> 00:37:32,279
you're flying on an airline today, as much as,

518
00:37:32,391 --> 00:37:35,645
you know, 95 to 98% of that flight is flown completely autonomous.

519
00:37:35,741 --> 00:37:39,106
You know, it's typically just the landing and the takeoff, you know, where the

520
00:37:39,606 --> 00:37:43,273
pilot, you know, has the controls. Everything else is done, you know, through waypoints,

521
00:37:43,289 --> 00:37:47,022
computers, and everything else to make sure that

522
00:37:47,522 --> 00:37:51,335
that we're doing things safely and whatnot. The 4th category is aerodynamics.

523
00:37:51,383 --> 00:37:54,894
And so you can see from this middle frame, the top picture in the middle

524
00:37:55,394 --> 00:37:58,565
frame, through advanced manufacturing, composite materials,

525
00:37:58,757 --> 00:38:01,450
and just AI and computers,

